  "finding": {
   "question": "Does the 1981 horror film Ghostkeeper derive its plot from the Wendigo legend?",
   "answer": "INSUFFICIENT — The supplied search results do not indicate whether Ghostkeeper derives its plot from the Wendigo legend.",
   "source": "",
   "excerpt": "",
   "stance": "INSUFFICIENT"
  },
  "passage": ""
 },
 {
  "finding": {
   "question": "Is the 1981 horror film Ghostkeeper's plot inspired by the Wendigo legend?",
   "answer": "Yes — the film is inspired by the Windigo legend of North America.",
   "source": "Ghostkeeper - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghostkeeper",
   "excerpt": "The film is inspired by the Windigo legend of North America.",
   "stance": "SUPPORTS"
  },
  "passage": "Title: List of monster movies\nURL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_monster_movies\nExcerpt: This is a list of monster movies, about such creatures as extraterrestrial aliens, giant animals, Kaiju (the Japanese counterpart of giant animals, but they can also be machines and plants), mutants, supernatural creatures, or creatures from folklore, such as Bigfoot or the Loch Ness Monster. These movies usually fall into the science fiction, fantasy and/or horror genres.\n\nTitle: Ghostkeeper\nURL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghostkeeper\nExcerpt: Ghostkeeper is a 1981 Canadian supernatural slasher film directed by James Makichuk, and starring Riva Spier, Georgie Collins, and Murray Ord. Its plot centers on a trio of snowmobilers in the Canadian Rockies who become stranded at an abandoned hotel where the elderly female innkeeper is hiding an evil entity within the building. The film is inspired by the Windigo legend of North America.\nFilmed in Banff, Alberta under a tax shelter in December 1980, the film had an unstable financial situation and the filmmakers nearly halted the production mid-way through due to depletion of its budget. The film was given a brief theatrical release in Canada and was released directly to video in the United States by New World Pictures. It has attained a cult following in the years since its release.\nThe independent cult label Code Red Releasing later acquired the film, and released on DVD for the first time in April 2012. In August 2017, they reissued the film on Blu-ray featuring a new 2K scan of the original film elements."
 },
 {
  "finding": {
   "question": "Is \"Wendigo psychosis\" listed as an official diagnosis in the DSM or ICD?",
   "answer": "INSUFFICIENT — The supplied search results mention 'Wendigo psychosis' in modern psychiatry but do not state whether it is an official DSM or ICD diagnosis.",
   "source": "Wendigo - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wendigo",
   "excerpt": "In modern psychiatry, the disorder known as \"Wendigo psychosis\" is characterized by symptoms such as an intense craving for human flesh and fear of becoming a cannibal.",
   "stance": "INSUFFICIENT"
  },
  "passage": ""
 }
]

